# 4. Mathematical foundations

## 4.1 Projection onto a single vector

Let (x) be the vector we want to project and (u) the target direction.

The projected vector must have the form:

[
p=\alpha u
]

where (\alpha) is a scalar.

The residual is:

[
r=x-p=x-\alpha u
]

For an orthogonal projection, the residual must be perpendicular to (u):

[
u^\top(x-\alpha u)=0
]

Expanding:

[
u^\top x-\alpha u^\top u=0
]

Solving for (\alpha):

[
\alpha=
\frac{u^\top x}{u^\top u}
]

Therefore:

[
\boxed{
\operatorname{proj}_u(x)
========================

\frac{u^\top x}{u^\top u}u
}
]

Where:

* (x) is the original vector;
* (u) is the target direction;
* (u^\top x) is the dot product;
* (u^\top u=|u|^2);
* (\alpha) is the coordinate of (x) along (u).

If (u) has unit norm:

[
u^\top u=1
]

and the formula becomes:

[
\operatorname{proj}_u(x)=(u^\top x)u
]

---

## 4.2 Projection as an optimization problem

Projection can also be defined as the closest point in the target subspace.

For a line generated by (u):

[
\alpha^\star
============

\arg\min_\alpha
|x-\alpha u|^2
]

The objective is:

[
f(\alpha)
=========

(x-\alpha u)^\top(x-\alpha u)
]

Expanding:

[
f(\alpha)
=========

x^\top x
-2\alpha u^\top x
+\alpha^2u^\top u
]

Differentiate with respect to (\alpha):

[
\frac{df}{d\alpha}
==================

-2u^\top x+2\alpha u^\top u
]

Setting the derivative to zero:

[
-2u^\top x+2\alpha u^\top u=0
]

Therefore:

[
\alpha=
\frac{u^\top x}{u^\top u}
]

The geometric and optimization interpretations produce the same result.

---

## 4.3 Projection onto a subspace

Suppose the target subspace is spanned by the columns of:

[
A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times k}
]

Any vector in the column space of (A) can be represented as:

[
A\beta
]

where:

[
\beta\in\mathbb{R}^k
]

We want to find the closest point to (x):

[
\beta^\star
===========

\arg\min_\beta
|x-A\beta|^2
]

The residual must be orthogonal to all columns of (A):

[
A^\top(x-A\beta)=0
]

Expanding:

[
A^\top x-A^\top A\beta=0
]

This produces the normal equations:

[
A^\top A\beta=A^\top x
]

If (A) has full column rank:

[
\beta^\star=
(A^\top A)^{-1}A^\top x
]

The projected vector is:

[
\hat{x}=A\beta^\star
]

Therefore:

[
\boxed{
\hat{x}
=======

A(A^\top A)^{-1}A^\top x
}
]

The projection matrix is:

[
\boxed{
P=A(A^\top A)^{-1}A^\top
}
]

so that:

[
\hat{x}=Px
]

---

## 4.4 Properties of a projection matrix

An orthogonal projection matrix satisfies:

[
P^\top=P
]

This means it is symmetric.

It also satisfies:

[
P^2=P
]

This property is called **idempotence**.

Once a vector has already been projected into the subspace, applying the projection again changes nothing:

[
P(Px)=Px
]

---

## 4.5 Projection with an orthonormal basis

If the columns of (Q) form an orthonormal basis:

[
Q^\top Q=I
]

then the projection matrix simplifies to:

[
P=QQ^\top
]

and:

[
\boxed{
\hat{x}=QQ^\top x
}
]

This operation can be interpreted in two steps.

First:

[
c=Q^\top x
]

computes the coordinates of (x) relative to the orthonormal basis.

Then:

[
\hat{x}=Qc
]

reconstructs the vector inside the subspace.

Together:

[
\hat{x}=Q(Q^\top x)
]

---

## 4.6 Residual and orthogonal decomposition

Every vector (x) can be decomposed as:

[
x=\hat{x}+r
]

where:

* (\hat{x}) lies in the selected subspace;
* (r=x-\hat{x}) lies in the orthogonal complement.

For an orthogonal projection:

[
\hat{x}^\top r=0
]

The Pythagorean relationship becomes:

[
|x|^2
=====

|\hat{x}|^2+|r|^2
]

The projected component represents the information retained by the subspace.

The residual represents the information discarded.

---

## 4.7 Relationship with least-squares regression

Consider linear regression:

[
y=X\beta+\epsilon
]

where:

* (y\in\mathbb{R}^n) is the target vector;
* (X\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times p}) is the design matrix;
* (\beta\in\mathbb{R}^p) contains the coefficients;
* (\epsilon) is the residual error.

Ordinary least squares solves:

[
\hat{\beta}
===========

\arg\min_\beta
|y-X\beta|^2
]

The fitted values are:

[
\hat{y}=X\hat{\beta}
]

Therefore, (\hat{y}) is the orthogonal projection of (y) onto the column space of (X).

The residual:

[
e=y-\hat{y}
]

is orthogonal to every feature column:

[
X^\top e=0
]

This is the geometric meaning of the normal equations.

---

## 4.8 Relationship with PCA

Let:

[
X\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}
]

be a centered data matrix.

PCA identifies orthonormal directions that retain the largest possible amount of variance.

Let:

[
W_k\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times k}
]

contain the first (k) principal directions.

The lower-dimensional coordinates are:

[
Z=XW_k
]

The reconstruction is:

[
\hat{X}=ZW_k^\top
]

Therefore:

[
\hat{X}=XW_kW_k^\top
]

The matrix:

[
W_kW_k^\top
]

projects the observations into the principal subspace.

PCA chooses the (k)-dimensional linear subspace that minimizes squared reconstruction error.

---

## 4.9 Rank and effective dimensionality

For a matrix:

[
A\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}
]

the rank is the dimension of its column space:

[
\operatorname{rank}(A)
======================

\dim(\operatorname{Col}(A))
]

It is also equal to the dimension of its row space.

Suppose an embedding matrix has shape:

[
N\times768
]

If its rank is 300, the observed embeddings span at most a 300-dimensional subspace.

In floating-point systems, exact rank may not be the most useful measure. Singular values are commonly inspected to estimate numerical or effective rank.

---

# 5. Practical applicability

## Dimensionality reduction

Projection is useful when high-dimensional data contains redundant, correlated or noisy directions.

Possible benefits include:

* faster training;
* lower storage requirements;
* faster nearest-neighbor search;
* visualization;
* noise reduction;
* reduced multicollinearity.

The main trade-off is information loss.

A lower-dimensional projection may preserve most global variance but discard low-variance information that is highly relevant to the target task.

---

## Embedding compression

Suppose a RAG system stores 768-dimensional embeddings.

A learned projection could reduce them to 256 dimensions.

Potential benefits:

* smaller vector index;
* lower memory usage;
* faster distance calculations;
* reduced storage cost;
* faster network transfer.

Potential costs:

* lower recall;
* changed nearest-neighbor rankings;
* altered similarity thresholds;
* distribution-shift sensitivity;
* additional transformation versioning.

The same transformation must be applied to:

* document embeddings;
* query embeddings;
* evaluation data.

Otherwise, the vectors will no longer belong to the same representation space.

---

## RAG and semantic search

Vector-space concepts explain why:

* query and document embeddings must be compatible;
* changing the embedding model requires re-embedding the documents;
* normalization affects similarity calculations;
* dimensionality affects vector-index cost;
* a projection may alter semantic neighborhoods;
* similarity thresholds are model-dependent.

A vector database such as FAISS, pgvector or Vertex AI Vector Search stores numerical vectors and performs nearest-neighbor retrieval.

The tool is an implementation detail. The underlying concept is geometric search inside a representation space.

---

## Feature redundancy and multicollinearity

When feature columns are linearly dependent or nearly dependent:

[
X^\top X
]

may become singular or poorly conditioned.

Consequences include:

* unstable coefficients;
* high estimator variance;
* sensitivity to small changes;
* difficulty interpreting individual feature effects.

Possible solutions include:

* removing redundant features;
* regularization;
* PCA;
* QR decomposition;
* singular value decomposition;
* domain-driven feature selection.

---

## Recommendation systems

Matrix factorization represents users and items in a shared latent space.

A predicted interaction may be calculated as:

[
\hat{r}_{ui}=p_u^\top q_i
]

where:

* (p_u) is the user vector;
* (q_i) is the item vector.

The latent basis is learned.

Individual coordinates are usually not uniquely interpretable. Rotating the latent space may change those coordinates while preserving the dot products and predictions.

---

## Removing a direction from embeddings

Given a normalized direction (u), its component can be removed from vector (x):

[
x_{\text{clean}}
================

x-\operatorname{proj}_u(x)
]

For unit (u):

[
x_{\text{clean}}
================

x-(u^\top x)u
]

This may be useful for reducing a nuisance signal or studying a semantic direction.

However, semantic properties are often distributed and nonlinear. Removing one direction does not guarantee that the concept has been removed from the representation.

---

## When linear projection may not be appropriate

Linear projection may be insufficient when:

* the data lies on a nonlinear manifold;
* important information is contained in low-variance directions;
* the distribution changes significantly;
* the target relationship is nonlinear;
* original feature interpretability must be preserved;
* the projection was fitted on an unrepresentative sample.

Possible nonlinear alternatives include:

* kernel PCA;
* autoencoders;
* UMAP;
* neural projection heads;
* manifold-learning methods.

---

# 6. Common pitfalls and mistakes

## Confusing a vector with its coordinates

The vector is the abstract geometric object.

The coordinates are its representation relative to a basis.

A change of basis changes the coordinates, not the underlying vector.

---

## Assuming every spanning set is a basis

A spanning set may contain redundant vectors.

A basis must both:

* span the space;
* be linearly independent.

---

## Confusing ambient and intrinsic dimension

A dataset represented using 1,000 features does not necessarily contain 1,000 independent directions of information.

---

## Computing a matrix inverse explicitly

The mathematical formula:

[
(A^\top A)^{-1}A^\top x
]

is useful for derivation, but explicitly calculating the inverse is generally poor numerical practice.

Prefer:

* `numpy.linalg.lstsq`;
* QR decomposition;
* SVD;
* pseudoinverse when appropriate.

---

## Ignoring rank deficiency

If the columns of (A) are linearly dependent:

[
A^\top A
]

is singular.

A conventional inverse does not exist.

---

## Forgetting to center data before PCA

Without centering, PCA may primarily capture the offset of the data from the origin rather than its meaningful variation.

---

## Data leakage

A learned projection such as PCA must be fitted only on training data.

Correct process:

1. fit on training data;
2. transform training data;
3. apply the fitted transformation to validation data;
4. apply the same transformation to test and production data.

---

## Treating PCA as feature selection

PCA creates new features that are linear combinations of the originals.

It does not simply select a subset of the original columns.

---

## Assuming cosine and Euclidean distance are always equivalent

For normalized vectors:

[
|x-y|^2
=======

2-2x^\top y
]

because:

[
|x|=|y|=1
]

In this case, ranking by Euclidean distance is equivalent to ranking by cosine similarity.

Without normalization, the rankings may differ substantially.

---

## Interpreting individual embedding dimensions

A specific dimension usually does not correspond to a clean concept such as “legal meaning” or “sentiment.”

Meaning is normally distributed across combinations of dimensions.

---

## Evaluating embedding compression only with explained variance

Explained variance and reconstruction error do not directly measure retrieval quality.

For RAG, also evaluate:

* recall@k;
* precision@k;
* mean reciprocal rank;
* nDCG;
* relevant-context coverage;
* answer correctness;
* latency;
* index size;
* cost.

---

# 7. Important comparisons

## Basis versus spanning set

A spanning set can represent every vector in a space but may contain redundancy.

A basis is a linearly independent spanning set.

---

## Orthogonal versus non-orthogonal basis

With an orthonormal basis (Q), coordinates are:

[
c=Q^\top x
]

With a general non-orthogonal basis (B), coordinates require solving:

[
Bc=x
]

An orthonormal basis generally provides simpler and more stable calculations.

---

## Projection versus general linear transformation

A general linear transformation has the form:

[
T(x)=Ax
]

It may:

* rotate;
* scale;
* reflect;
* shear;
* compress;
* expand.

A projection is a special linear transformation satisfying:

[
P^2=P
]

---

## Orthogonal versus oblique projection

An orthogonal projection generates a residual perpendicular to the target subspace.

Its matrix satisfies:

[
P^\top=P
]

An oblique projection projects along a direction that is not perpendicular to the target subspace.

It still satisfies:

[
P^2=P
]

but generally:

[
P^\top\neq P
]

---

## PCA versus random projection

### PCA

* learns directions from the data;
* maximizes retained variance;
* must be fitted;
* is sensitive to distribution changes;
* provides data-dependent components.

### Random projection

* uses a random transformation;
* is cheaper to construct;
* may approximately preserve pairwise distances;
* does not optimize variance;
* can be useful at very large scale.

PCA is useful when variance retention and reconstruction matter.

Random projection is attractive when scalability and approximate distance preservation are more important.

---

## PCA versus feature selection

PCA creates transformed features.

Feature selection preserves a subset of the original variables.

Use PCA when compression and decorrelation matter.

Use feature selection when maintaining original feature semantics is important.

---

## Euclidean distance versus cosine similarity

Euclidean distance measures absolute separation:

[
d(x,y)=|x-y|
]

Cosine similarity measures directional alignment:

[
\cos(\theta)
============

\frac{x^\top y}{|x||y|}
]

Cosine similarity ignores magnitude.

Whether magnitude is relevant depends on the model and training objective.

---

## SVD versus eigendecomposition

Eigendecomposition is defined directly for square matrices.

Singular value decomposition can be applied to any matrix:

[
A=U\Sigma V^\top
]

SVD is central to:

* PCA;
* rank estimation;
* pseudoinverses;
* stable least-squares solutions;
* low-rank approximations.

